  Harrow by eleven."

  Walt looked at the [3mBluebell[0m for a long
  moment. Her bell had gone green.
  Somebody had written WE'LL MISS YOU in
  the grime on her saloon window with a
  finger.

  "No," he said. "We're taking the long
  way."

  Teo frowned. "There isn't a long way."

  "There is today."

  They made fast at seven and eased her
  off the pier, the [3mHester[0m leaning into
  the hawser with that low, stubborn
  growl. The ferry came along behind
  them like an old dog on a lead, a bit
  reluctant but willing to be persuaded.
  Instead of turning south toward the
  channel, Walt swung north toward Gull
  Point.

  Gull Point was the ferry's first stop.
  It always had been, at 7:20 every
  weekday, rain or shine. Walt eased the
  throttle back as they came abreast of
  the little landing and reached for
  the horn cord. Two short blasts, one
  long: the [3mBluebell[0m's old arrival
  signal, the one every child on the

